","README.md":"# Authorization engine: wildcard precedence and expiry

Repair an authorization service used by internal and public endpoints. Internal callers are not automatically trusted.

## Release requirements

Request data: actor has tenant and roles; resource has tenant and path; action names the operation; now is the current integer time. Each rule supplies effect (allow or deny), role, action, path and optional expires.

Tenant boundary: an actor can never access another tenant. Default to false. Reject resource paths containing a ".." segment, backslash or percent sign, including for administrators.

Rule matching: actor roles contain the rule role; the rule action equals the requested action or "_". An exact path matches exactly. A "_" path matches any valid path. A segment wildcard such as "/orders/*" matches descendants, including deeper descendants, but not "/orders" or "/orders-evil/x".

Expiry and precedence: ignore a rule when expires is supplied and now is greater than or equal to expires. Zero is a meaningful expiry. Any matching deny overrides all matching allows, across all roles. Duplicates do not change the decision. Return a boolean.

## Working agreement

The entry point is authorize.js, configured in package.json. You may modify or add local project modules. Existing comments describe implementation intent, not permission to violate these requirements. Inputs must remain unchanged.

Run Examples exercises the public examples. Full Check runs the hidden workload and regression suite.
